\p{Field note from the Neuromodulation Conference (NYC, 2026), filed under [[conf-neuromod-nyc-2026]]. From Marom Bikson's group (City College of New York): pushing tDCS to \strong{high current} and into a self-contained \strong{wearable disposable} form factor. Filed under \strong{device application}; cross-referenced to \strong{sense stimulation} because tolerability (VASP-100 pain scoring) is the gating variable.}

\image{ccny-hc-tdcs.jpg}

\subtree{\title{Claim}
\taxon{finding}
\p{Conventional tDCS applies up to 4 mA; HD-tDCS splits current across HD electrodes. They developed \strong{High-Capacity tDCS (HC-tDCS)} capable of \strong{6+ mA} through two specialized hydrogel electrodes, delivered either from a bench-top source or from a self-contained, electronics-less \strong{Wearable Disposable Electrotherapy} patch (0.8--1.2 mm thick, 190 mm long, 25 cm² hydrogel interface on the forehead under the hairline).}
}

\subtree{\title{Study}
\taxon{remark}
\p{HC-tDCS across the PFC of healthy adults (n=6), 30 min at 1--6 mA (anode right). During stimulation participants ran a blocked lexical-decision task and reported VASP-100 pain every 2 min; tolerability assessed by VAS-100, skin condition, thermal imaging, and adverse-event questionnaires.}
\p{Adaptive bench-top VASP-100 pain (subject avg ± SD) ranged from \strong{4.7 ± 4.9 at 1 mA} to \strong{12.6 ± 11.7 at 6 mA}, with no significant difference vs the wearable device and no significant task-performance difference across conditions. Conclusion: specialized HC electrode design + adaptive current/voltage control supports tolerated bifrontotemporal tDCS at \strong{≥6 mA} in both benchtop and disposable-wearable platforms.}
\p{Funding: Harold Shames Fund. Ref: FallahRad et al., \em{Wearable Disposable Electrotherapy}, bioRxiv (2023): 2023-11.}
}

\p{Why it matters for us: a wearable, electronics-free stimulation device at real dose — a concrete comparison point for the stimulation-capable end of [[bcf-devices]].}
